{:edb/db-before-t 1127 :edb/db-after-t 1128 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534440 50 #inst "2026-10-11T20:48:10.401Z" 13194139534440 17592186045417 true] [17592186151031 1078 #edb/ref 17592186151034 13194139534440 17592186045417 true] [17592186151849 1071 "Consider the equivalence relation $\\sim$ on $\\mathbb{Z}$, defined by $x \\sim y$ if $12 \\, \\mid \\, (x-y)$. Which of the following are in the equivalence class $[9]$?\n\n1. $9$\n2. $21$\n3. $22$" 13194139534440 17592186045417 false] [17592186151849 1071 "Consider the equivalence relation $\\sim$ on $\\mathbb{Z},$ defined by $x \\sim y$ if $12 \\,|\\, (x-y).$ Which of the following are in the equivalence class $[9]?$\n\nI. $9$\n\nII. $21$\n\nIII. $22$" 13194139534440 17592186045417 true] [17592186151849 1074 "The equivalence class $[9]$ consists of the elements that are equivalent to $9$ under the given equivalence relation. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n[9] &= {x ∈ Z: x∼9} \\\\\n&= {x ∈ Z: 12 \\mid (x - 9)} \\\\\n&= {x ∈ Z: x = 9 + 12k, k ∈ Z} \\\\\n&= {…,-15,-3, 9, 21, 33, …}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe see that $9$ and $21$ are in $[9]$, while $22$ is not.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139534440 17592186045417 false] [17592186151849 1074 "The equivalence class $[9]$ consists of the elements that are equivalent to $9$ under the given equivalence relation. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[9] & ={x\\in Z\\,:\\,x∼9} \\\\  & ={x\\in Z\\,:\\,12\\,|\\,(x-9)} \\\\  & ={x\\in Z\\,:\\,x=9+12k,k\\in Z} \\\\  & ={…,-15,-3,9,21,33,…}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe see that $9$ and $21$ are in $[9],$ while $22$ is not.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139534440 17592186045417 true] [17592186151851 1071 "Consider the equivalence relation $\\sim$ on $\\mathbb{R}$, defined by $x \\sim y$ if $\\sin x = \\sin y$. Which of the following equivalence classes contain the element $\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}$?\n\n1. $\\left[\\dfrac{3\\pi}{2}\\right]$\n2. $\\left[-\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right]$\n3. $\\left[-\\dfrac{3\\pi}{2}\\right]$" 13194139534440 17592186045417 false] [17592186151851 1071 "Consider the equivalence relation $\\sim$ on $\\mathbb{R},$ defined by $x \\sim y$ if $\\sin x = \\sin y.$ Which of the following equivalence classes contain the element $\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}?$\n\nI. $\\left[\\dfrac{3\\pi}{2}\\right]$\n\nII. $\\left[-\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right]$\n\nIII. $\\left[-\\dfrac{3\\pi}{2}\\right]$" 13194139534440 17592186045417 true] [17592186151851 1074 "We have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\pi}{2} \\in [x]\n$$\n\nif\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\pi}{2} \\sim x\n$$\n\nUsing the definition of the given equivalence relation, we require\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sin (\\frac{π}{2}) &= \\sin x \\\\\n1 &= \\sin x \\\\\n\\sin x &= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now consider each of the given equivalence classes.\n\n- $\\dfrac{\\pi}{2} \\not\\in \\left[\\dfrac{3\\pi}{2}\\right]$ because $\\sin\\left(\\dfrac{3\\pi}{2}\\right) =-1\\neq 1 \\quad \\color{red}\\times$\n- $\\dfrac{\\pi}{2} \\not\\in \\left[-\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right]$ because $\\sin\\left(-\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right) =-1\\neq 1 \\quad \\color{red}\\times$\n- $\\dfrac{\\pi}{2} \\in \\left[-\\dfrac{3\\pi}{2}\\right]$ because $\\sin \\left(-\\dfrac{3\\pi}{2}\\right) = 1 \\quad \\color{green}\\checkmark$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"." 13194139534440 17592186045417 false] [17592186151851 1074 "We have $\\dfrac{\\pi}{2} \\in [x]$ if $\\dfrac{\\pi}{2} \\sim x.$ Using the definition of the given equivalence relation, we require\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ 1 & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now consider each of the given equivalence classes.\n\n- $\\dfrac{\\pi}{2} \\not\\in \\left[\\dfrac{3\\pi}{2}\\right]$ because $\\sin\\left(\\dfrac{3\\pi}{2}\\right) =-1\\neq 1 \\quad \\color{red}\\times$\n\n- $\\dfrac{\\pi}{2} \\not\\in \\left[-\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right]$ because $\\sin\\left(-\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right) =-1\\neq 1 \\quad \\color{red}\\times$\n\n- $\\dfrac{\\pi}{2} \\in \\left[-\\dfrac{3\\pi}{2}\\right]$ because $\\sin \\left(-\\dfrac{3\\pi}{2}\\right) = 1  \\quad \\color{green}\\checkmark$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"." 13194139534440 17592186045417 true] [17592186151852 1071 "Consider the equivalence relation $\\sim$ on the set $A = \\{0, 1, 2, \\ldots, 9\\}$, defined by $a \\sim b$ if $\\textrm{i}^a = \\textrm{i}^b$, where $\\textrm{i}$ is the imaginary unit in $\\mathbb{C}$. How many distinct equivalence classes does $\\sim$ partition $A$ into?" 13194139534440 17592186045417 false] [17592186151852 1071 "Consider the equivalence relation $\\sim$ on the set $A = \\{0, 1, 2, \\ldots, 9\\},$ defined by $a \\sim b$ if $\\textrm{i}^a = \\textrm{i}^b,$ where $\\textrm{i}$ is the imaginary unit in $\\mathbb{C}.$  How many distinct equivalence classes does $\\sim$ partition $A$ into?" 13194139534440 17592186045417 true] [17592186151852 1074 "The equivalence classes of $\\sim$ partition $A$, meaning that any element $a \\in A$ belongs to exactly one distinct equivalence class.\n\nSo, we compute equivalence classes until all the elements of $A$ have been placed into an equivalence class.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n[0] &= {a ∈ A∣a∼0} \\\\\n&= {a ∈ A∣\\textrm{i}^{a} = \\textrm{i}^{0}} \\\\\n&= {a ∈ A∣\\textrm{i}^{a} = 1} \\\\\n&= {0, 4, 8} \\\\\n[1] &= {a ∈ A∣a∼1} \\\\\n&= {a ∈ A∣\\textrm{i}^{a} = \\textrm{i}^{1}} \\\\\n&= {a ∈ A∣\\textrm{i}^{a} = \\textrm{i}} \\\\\n&= {1, 5, 9} \\\\\n[2] &= {a ∈ A∣a∼2} \\\\\n&= {a ∈ A∣\\textrm{i}^{a} = \\textrm{i}^{2}} \\\\\n&= {a ∈ A∣\\textrm{i}^{a} =-1} \\\\\n&= {2, 6} \\\\\n[3] &= {a ∈ A∣a∼3} \\\\\n&= {a ∈ A∣\\textrm{i}^{a} = \\textrm{i}^{3}} \\\\\n&= {a ∈ A∣\\textrm{i}^{a} =-\\textrm{i}} \\\\\n&= {3, 7}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEach element of $A$ has been placed into an equivalence class, so we are done.\n\nWe have $4$ distinct equivalence classes:\n\n$$\n[0] = \\{0, 4, 8 \\}, \\quad [1] = \\{1, 5, 9 \\}, \\quad [2] = \\{2, 6 \\}, \\quad [3] = \\{3, 7 \\}\n$$" 13194139534440 17592186045417 false] [17592186151852 1074 "The equivalence classes of $\\sim$ partition $A,$ meaning that any element $a \\in A$ belongs to exactly one distinct equivalence class.\n\nSo, we compute equivalence classes until all the elements of $A$ have been placed into an equivalence class.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[0] & ={a\\in A∣a∼0} \\\\  & ={a\\in A∣{\\text{i}}^{a}={\\text{i}}^{0}} \\\\  & ={a\\in A∣{\\text{i}}^{a}=1} \\\\  & ={0,4,8} \\\\  \\\\ [1] & ={a\\in A∣a∼1} \\\\  & ={a\\in A∣{\\text{i}}^{a}={\\text{i}}^{1}} \\\\  & ={a\\in A∣{\\text{i}}^{a}=\\text{i}} \\\\  & ={1,5,9} \\\\  \\\\ [2] & ={a\\in A∣a∼2} \\\\  & ={a\\in A∣{\\text{i}}^{a}={\\text{i}}^{2}} \\\\  & ={a\\in A∣{\\text{i}}^{a}=-1} \\\\  & ={2,6} \\\\  \\\\ [3] & ={a\\in A∣a∼3} \\\\  & ={a\\in A∣{\\text{i}}^{a}={\\text{i}}^{3}} \\\\  & ={a\\in A∣{\\text{i}}^{a}=-\\text{i}} \\\\  & ={3,7}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEach element of $A$ has been placed into an equivalence class, so we are done.\n\nWe have $4$ distinct equivalence classes:\n\n$$\n[0] = \\{ 0, 4, 8 \\} , \\quad [1] = \\{ 1, 5, 9 \\}, \\quad [2] = \\{ 2, 6 \\}, \\quad [3] = \\{ 3, 7 \\}.\n$$" 13194139534440 17592186045417 true] [17592186152256 1038 #edb/ref 17592186421716 13194139534440 17592186045417 true] [17592186152256 1038 #edb/ref 17592186421717 13194139534440 17592186045417 true] [17592186152256 1038 #edb/ref 17592186421718 13194139534440 17592186045417 true] [17592186152256 1038 #edb/ref 17592186421722 13194139534440 17592186045417 true] [17592186152256 1038 #edb/ref 17592186421723 13194139534440 17592186045417 true] [17592186152257 1038 #edb/ref 17592186421719 13194139534440 17592186045417 true] [17592186152257 1038 #edb/ref 17592186421721 13194139534440 17592186045417 true] [17592186152257 1038 #edb/ref 17592186421724 13194139534440 17592186045417 true] [17592186152257 1038 #edb/ref 17592186421729 13194139534440 17592186045417 true] [17592186152258 1038 #edb/ref 17592186421720 13194139534440 17592186045417 true] [17592186152258 1038 #edb/ref 17592186421725 13194139534440 17592186045417 true] [17592186152258 1038 #edb/ref 17592186421726 13194139534440 17592186045417 true] [17592186152258 1038 #edb/ref 17592186421727 13194139534440 17592186045417 true] [17592186152258 1038 #edb/ref 17592186421728 13194139534440 17592186045417 true] [17592186152337 1050 660.0 13194139534440 17592186045417 true] [17592186152754 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534440 17592186045417 true] [17592186152754 1071 "Consider the equivalence relation $∼$ on $C$, defined by $x∼y$ if $x^{4} = y^{4}$. Which of the following equivalence classes contain the element $\\textrm{i}$?\n\n1. $[- 1]$\n2. $[- \\textrm{i}]$\n3. $[2\\textrm{i}]$" 13194139534440 17592186045417 false] [17592186152754 1071 "Consider the equivalence relation $∼$ on $C,$ defined by $x∼y$ if ${x}^{4}={y}^{4}.$ Which of the following equivalence classes contain the element $i?$\n\nI. $[-1]$\n\nII. $[-i]$\n\nIII. $[2i]$" 13194139534440 17592186045417 true] [17592186152754 1074 "We have $i\\in [x]$ if $i∼x.$ Using the definition of the given equivalence relation, we require\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{i}^{4} & ={x}^{4} \\\\ 1 & ={x}^{4} \\\\ {x}^{4} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now consider each of the given equivalence classes.\n\n- $i\\in [-1]$ because $(-1)^{4}=1\\,✓$\n\n- $i\\in [-i]$ because $(-i)^{4}=1\\,✓$\n\n- $i\\notin [2i]$ because $(2i)^{4}=16\\ne 1\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only\"." 13194139534440 17592186045417 true] [17592186153457 1064 "Recall that the residue class of $a$ modulo $n$, denoted $[a]_n$, is the set of integers that are congruent to $a$ modulo $n$.\n\n$$\n[a]_n = \\big\\{x \\in \\mathbb{Z} \\: \\mid \\: x \\equiv a \\, \\: (\\text{mod}\\:n) \\big\\}\n$$\n\nFor example:\n\n- The residue class of $0$ modulo $2$ corresponds to the even integers because all even integers have a remainder of $0$ when divided by $2$.\n$[0]_{2} = {0, ± 2, ± 4, …}$\n- On the other hand, the residue class of $1$ modulo $2$ corresponds to the odd integers because all odd integers have a remainder of $1$ when divided by $2$.\n$[1]_{2} = {± 1, ± 3, ± 5, …}$\n\nNotice that the residue classes $[0]_2$ and $[1]_2$ *partition* the integers, meaning that any integer belongs either to $[0]_2$ or $[1]_2$, but never both.\n\n![](images/89/8932542ec22de980b3b2e1a5a11a3edf2250efc7ea4f1ca64ca35aa4031c63c9.png)" 13194139534440 17592186045417 false] [17592186153457 1064 "Recall that the residue class of $a$ modulo $n,$ denoted $[a]_n,$ is the set of integers that are congruent to $a$ modulo $n.$\n\n$$\n[a]_n = \\big\\{ x \\in \\mathbb{Z} \\: | \\: x \\equiv a \\,  \\: (\\text{mod}\\:n) \\big\\}\n$$\n\nFor example:\n\n- The residue class of $0$ modulo $2$ corresponds to the even integers because all even integers have a remainder of $0$ when divided by $2.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[0]_{2}={0,\\,\\pm 2,\\,\\pm 4,\\,…}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- On the other hand, the residue class of $1$ modulo $2$ corresponds to the odd integers because all odd integers have a remainder of $1$ when divided by $2.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[1]_{2}={\\,\\pm 1,\\,\\pm 3,\\,\\pm 5,\\,…}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the residue classes $[0]_2$ and $[1]_2$ *partition* the integers, meaning that any integer belongs either to $[0]_2$ or $[1]_2,$ but never both.\n\n![](images/89/8932542ec22de980b3b2e1a5a11a3edf2250efc7ea4f1ca64ca35aa4031c63c9.png)" 13194139534440 17592186045417 true] [17592186153458 1064 "A residue class is a particular type of a more general object called an **equivalence class.**\n\nGiven an equivalence relation $\\sim$ on a set $A$, the equivalence class of an element $a \\in A$, denoted $[a]$, consists of all the elements of $A$ that are equivalent to $a$.\n\n$$\n[a] = \\{x \\in A \\, \\mid \\, x \\sim a \\}\n$$\n\nSince any equivalence relation is symmetric, we can also write\n\n$$\n[a] = \\{x \\in A \\, \\mid \\, a \\sim x \\}\n$$\n\nFor example, consider the equivalence relation $\\sim$ on $\\mathbb{Z}$ defined by $a \\sim b$ if and only if\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{vmatrix}a &= & b\\end{vmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUnder this relation, two integers are equivalent if they have the same absolute value. The first few equivalence classes are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n[0] &= {a ∈ Z∣a∼0} \\\\\n= {a ∈ Z∣\\begin{vmatrix}a &= & 0\\end{vmatrix}} \\\\\n= {a ∈ Z∣\\begin{vmatrix}a &= 0} \\\\ = {0} \\\\ [1]|= {a ∈ Z∣a∼1} \\\\ = {a ∈ Z∣\\begin{vmatrix}a|= & 1\\end{vmatrix}} \\\\ = {a ∈ Z∣ \\mid a|= 1} \\\\ = {± 1} \\\\ [2]|= {a ∈ Z∣a∼2} \\\\ = {a ∈ Z∣\\begin{vmatrix}a|= & 2\\end{vmatrix}} \\\\ = {a ∈ Z∣ \\mid a|= 2} \\\\ = {± 2} \\\\ [3]|= {a ∈ Z∣a∼3} \\\\ = {a ∈ Z∣\\begin{vmatrix}a|= & 3\\end{vmatrix}} \\\\ = {a ∈ Z∣\\end{vmatrix}a|= 3} \\\\\n&= {± 3} \\\\\n&= ⋮\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- Equivalence relations can have a finite or infinite number of equivalence classes. This particular equivalence relation has an infinite number of equivalence classes.\n- For equivalence classes containing more than one element, we can use any element to represent the class. For example, $[1]$ and $[-1]$ represent the same equivalence class." 13194139534440 17592186045417 false] [17592186153458 1064 "A residue class is a particular type of a more general object called an **equivalence class.**\n\nGiven an equivalence relation $\\sim$ on a set $A,$ the equivalence class of an element $a \\in A,$ denoted $[a],$ consists of all the elements of $A$ that are equivalent to $a.$\n\n$$\n[a] = \\{ x \\in A \\, | \\, x \\sim a \\}\n$$\n\nSince any equivalence relation is symmetric, we can also write\n\n$$\n[a] = \\{ x \\in A \\, | \\, a \\sim x \\}.\n$$\n\nFor example, consider the equivalence relation $\\sim$ on $\\mathbb{Z}$ defined by $a \\sim b$ if and only if $|a|=|b|.$ \n\nUnder this relation, two integers are equivalent if they have the same absolute value. The first few equivalence classes are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[0] & ={a\\in Z∣a∼0} \\\\  & ={a\\in Z∣|a|=|0|} \\\\  & ={a\\in Z∣|a|=0} \\\\  & ={0} \\\\  \\\\ [1] & ={a\\in Z∣a∼1} \\\\  & ={a\\in Z∣|a|=|1|} \\\\  & ={a\\in Z∣|a|=1} \\\\  & ={\\pm 1} \\\\  \\\\ [2] & ={a\\in Z∣a∼2} \\\\  & ={a\\in Z∣|a|=|2|} \\\\  & ={a\\in Z∣|a|=2} \\\\  & ={\\pm 2} \\\\  \\\\ [3] & ={a\\in Z∣a∼3} \\\\  & ={a\\in Z∣|a|=|3|} \\\\  & ={a\\in Z∣|a|=3} \\\\  & ={\\pm 3} \\\\  & \\,⋮\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- Equivalence relations can have a finite or infinite number of equivalence classes. This particular equivalence relation has an infinite number of equivalence classes.\n\n- For equivalence classes containing more than one element, we can use any element to represent the class. For example, $[1]$ and $[-1]$ represent the same equivalence class." 13194139534440 17592186045417 true] [17592186153480 1064 "The properties of residue classes generalize to equivalence classes. Let's list some of them:\n\n- If $a \\sim b$ then $[a]=[b]$. This allows us to pick any element of a class as its representative.\n- On the other hand, two distinct equivalence classes have an empty intersection:\n$a \\not\\sim b \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad [a] \\cap [b] = \\emptyset$\n- The residue classes form a partition of $A$. Moreover, $A$ can be constructed from the (disjoint) union of all distinct equivalence classes:\n$A = \\bigcup_{i\\in I}\\,[a]_i$\nwhere $[a]_i$ denotes the $i$'th residue class and $I$ is an index set spanning all possible classes.\n\nConsider once again the equivalence relation $\\sim$ on $\\mathbb{Z}$ defined by $a \\sim b$ if and only if\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{vmatrix}a &= & b\\end{vmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe found that the equivalence classes were as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n[0] &= {0} \\\\\n[1] &= {± 1} \\\\\n[2] &= {± 2} \\\\\n[3] &= {± 3} \\\\\n&= ⋮\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThese equivalence classes form a partition of the set of integers:\n$\\big\\{[0], [1], [2],\\ldots\\big\\}$\n\nMoreover, the integers can be constructed from the disjoint union of these classes:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nZ &= [0]∪[1]∪[2]∪⋯ \\\\\n&= {0}∪{± 1}∪{± 2}∪⋯\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534440 17592186045417 false] [17592186153480 1064 "The properties of residue classes generalize to equivalence classes. Let's list some of them:\n\n- If $a \\sim b$ then $[a]=[b].$ This allows us to pick any element of a class as its representative.\n\n- On the other hand, two distinct equivalence classes have an empty intersection: \n\n$$\na \\not\\sim b\n\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\n[a] \\cap [b] = \\emptyset\n$$\n\n- The residue classes form a partition of $A.$ Moreover, $A$ can be constructed from the (disjoint) union of all distinct equivalence classes:\n\n$$\nA = \\bigcup_{i\\in I}\\,[a]_i\n$$\n\nwhere $[a]_i$ denotes the $i$'th residue class and $I$ is an index set spanning all possible classes.\n\nConsider once again the equivalence relation $\\sim$ on $\\mathbb{Z}$ defined by $a \\sim b$ if and only if $|a|=|b|.$ We found that the equivalence classes were as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[0] & ={0} \\\\ [1] & ={\\pm 1} \\\\ [2] & ={\\pm 2} \\\\ [3] & ={\\pm 3} \\\\  & ⋮\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThese equivalence classes form a partition of the set of integers:\n\n$$\n\\big\\{[0], [1], [2],\\ldots\\big\\}\n$$\n\nMoreover, the integers can be constructed from the disjoint union of these classes:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Z & =[0]\\cup [1]\\cup [2]\\cup ⋯ \\\\  & ={0}\\cup {\\pm 1}\\cup {\\pm 2}\\cup ⋯\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534440 17592186045417 true] [17592186421632 1080 "4" 13194139534440 17592186045417 true] [17592186421632 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534440 17592186045417 true] [17592186421632 1083 #uuid "03e6d699-08b0-544b-9709-1cd54a9ffc8e" 13194139534440 17592186045417 true] [17592186421633 1080 "5" 13194139534440 17592186045417 true] [17592186421633 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534440 17592186045417 true] [17592186421633 1083 #uuid "07ae0e