6421716 1074 "The equivalence class $[8]$ consists of the elements that are equivalent to $8$ under the given equivalence relation. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[8] & ={x\\in R\\,|\\,x∼8} \\\\  & ={x\\in R\\,|\\,{x}^{3}={8}^{3}} \\\\  & ={x\\in R\\,|\\,x=8} \\\\  & ={8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe see that $8$ is in $[8],$ while $-2$ and $2$ are not.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"." 13194139534440 17592186045417 true] [17592186421717 1068 #edb/ref 17592186421710 13194139534440 17592186045417 true] [17592186421717 1069 "q-221718" 13194139534440 17592186045417 true] [17592186421717 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139534440 17592186045417 true] [17592186421717 1071 "Consider the equivalence relation $∼$ on $Z,$ defined by $x∼y$ if $7\\,|\\,(x-y).$ Which of the following are in the equivalence class $[5]?$\n\nI. $12$\n\nII. $-4$\n\nIII. $-2$" 13194139534440 17592186045417 true] [17592186421717 1072 #uuid "22db406c-85ef-5a5a-ab22-d062645b4362" 13194139534440 17592186045417 true] [17592186421717 1074 "The equivalence class $[5]$ consists of the elements that are equivalent to $5$ under the given equivalence relation. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[5] & ={x\\in Z\\,:\\,x∼5} \\\\  & ={x\\in Z\\,:\\,7\\,|\\,(x-5)} \\\\  & ={x\\in Z\\,:\\,x=5+7k,k\\in Z} \\\\  & ={…,-9,-2,5,12,19,…}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe see that $12$ and $-2$ are in $[5],$ while $-4$ is not.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" 13194139534440 17592186045417 true] [17592186421718 1068 #edb/ref 17592186421704 13194139534440 17592186045417 true] [17592186421718 1069 "q-77205" 13194139534440 17592186045417 true] [17592186421718 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534440 17592186045417 true] [17592186421718 1071 "Consider the equivalence relation $∼$ on $C,$ defined by $x∼y$ if ${x}^{2}={y}^{2}.$ Which of the following are in the equivalence class $[-4]?$\n\nI. $-4$\n\nII. $4$\n\nIII. $4\\text{i}$" 13194139534440 17592186045417 true] [17592186421718 1072 #uuid "3c1db003-08b1-562e-8980-51389652bb7b" 13194139534440 17592186045417 true] [17592186421718 1074 "The equivalence class $[-4]$ consists of the elements that are equivalent to $-4$ under the given equivalence relation. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[-4] & ={x\\in C\\,|\\,x∼-4} \\\\  & ={x\\in C\\,|\\,{x}^{2}=(-4)^{2}} \\\\  & ={x\\in C\\,|\\,{x}^{2}=16} \\\\  & ={-4,4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe see that $-4$ and $4$ are in $[-4],$ while $4\\text{i}$ is not.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only\"." 13194139534440 17592186045417 true] [17592186421719 1068 #edb/ref 17592186421709 13194139534440 17592186045417 true] [17592186421719 1069 "q-221767" 13194139534440 17592186045417 true] [17592186421719 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534440 17592186045417 true] [17592186421719 1071 "Consider the equivalence relation $∼$ on $R,$ defined by $x∼y$ if $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi y.$ Which of the following equivalence classes contain the element $3$\n\nI. $[0]$\n\nII. $[1]$\n\nIII. $[2]$" 13194139534440 17592186045417 true] [17592186421719 1072 #uuid "46852a07-1e77-54f8-9037-1f17a12c391f" 13194139534440 17592186045417 true] [17592186421719 1074 "We have $3\\in [x]$ if $3∼x.$ Using the definition of the given equivalence relation, we require\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi \\cdot 3 & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi x \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡3\\pi  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi x \\\\ -1 & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi x \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi x & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now consider each of the given equivalence classes.\n\n- $3\\notin [0]$ because $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi \\cdot 0)=1\\ne -1\\,\\times $\n\n- $3\\in [1]$ because $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi \\cdot 1)=-1\\,✓$\n\n- $3\\notin [2]$ because $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi \\cdot 2)=1\\ne -1\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139534440 17592186045417 true] [17592186421720 1068 #edb/ref 17592186421714 13194139534440 17592186045417 true] [17592186421720 1069 "q-221800" 13194139534440 17592186045417 true] [17592186421720 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534440 17592186045417 true] [17592186421720 1071 "Consider the equivalence relation $∼$ on the set $A={0,\\,\\pm 1,\\,\\pm 2,\\,\\pm 3,\\,\\pm 4},$ defined by $x∼y$ if ${x}^{4}={y}^{4}.$ How many distinct equivalence classes does $∼$ partition $A$ into?" 13194139534440 17592186045417 true] [17592186421720 1072 #uuid "67dde538-2d6b-5e8f-8a86-3af3686c7b06" 13194139534440 17592186045417 true] [17592186421720 1074 "The equivalence classes of $∼$ partition $A,$ meaning that any element $a\\in A$ belongs to exactly one distinct equivalence class.\n\nSo, we compute equivalence classes until all the elements of $A$ have been placed into an equivalence class.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[0] & ={x\\in A∣x∼0} \\\\  & ={x\\in A∣{x}^{4}={0}^{4}} \\\\  & ={0} \\\\  \\\\ [1] & ={x\\in A∣x∼1} \\\\  & ={x\\in A∣{x}^{4}={1}^{4}} \\\\  & ={x\\in A∣{x}^{4}=1} \\\\  & ={-1,1} \\\\  \\\\ [2] & ={x\\in A∣x∼2} \\\\  & ={x\\in A∣{x}^{4}={2}^{4}} \\\\  & ={x\\in A∣{x}^{4}=16} \\\\  & ={-2,2} \\\\  \\\\ [3] & ={x\\in A∣x∼3} \\\\  & ={x\\in A∣{x}^{4}={3}^{4}} \\\\  & ={x\\in A∣{x}^{4}=81} \\\\  & ={-3,3} \\\\  \\\\ [4] & ={x\\in A∣x∼4} \\\\  & ={x\\in A∣{x}^{4}={4}^{4}} \\\\  & ={x\\in A∣{x}^{4}=256} \\\\  & ={-4,4} \\\\ \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAll of the elements of $A$ have been placed into an equivalence class, so we are done.\n\nWe have $5$ distinct equivalence classes:\n\n$$\n[0]={0},\\,[1]={-1,1},\\,[2]={-2,2},\\,[3]={-3,3},\\,[4]={-4,4}.\n$$\n\nNotice that the equivalence classes form a partition of $A,$ as expected." 13194139534440 17592186045417 true] [17592186421721 1068 #edb/ref 17592186421708 13194139534440 17592186045417 true] [17592186421721 1069 "q-221722" 13194139534440 17592186045417 true] [17592186421721 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534440 17592186045417 true] [17592186421721 1071 "Consider the equivalence relation $∼$ on $Z,$ defined by $x∼y$ if $11\\,|\\,(x-y).$ Which of the following equivalence classes contain the element $-6?$\n\nI. $[5]$\n\nII. $[-17]$\n\nIII. $[-8]$" 13194139534440 17592186045417 true] [17592186421721 1072 #uuid "9d844129-f571-5c80-934e-cfc1faad53cf" 13194139534440 17592186045417 true] [17592186421721 1074 "We have $-6\\in [x]$ if $-6∼x.$ Using the definition of the given equivalence relation, we require\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}11\\,|\\,(-6-x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now consider each of the given equivalence classes.\n\n- $-6\\in [5]$ because $11\\,|\\,(-6-5)\\,✓$\n\n- $-6\\in [-17]$ because $11\\,|\\,(-6+17)\\,✓$\n\n- $-6\\notin [-8]$ because $11∤(-6+8)\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139534440 17592186045417 true] [17592186421722 1068 #edb/ref 17592186421702 13194139534440 17592186045417 true] [17592186421722 1069 "q-221717" 13194139534440 17592186045417 true] [17592186421722 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534440 17592186045417 true] [17592186421722 1071 "Consider the equivalence relation $∼$ on $R,$ defined by $x∼y$ if $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡y.$ Which of the following are in the equivalence class $[0]?$\n\nI. $2\\pi $\n\nII. $3\\pi $\n\nIII. $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$" 13194139534440 17592186045417 true] [17592186421722 1072 #uuid "a9e1ee25-17a0-56ec-9f69-bcad0ae7d0cc" 13194139534440 17592186045417 true] [17592186421722 1074 "The equivalence class $[0]$ consists of the elements that are equivalent to $0$ under the given equivalence relation. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[0] & ={x\\in R\\,|\\,x∼0} \\\\  & ={x\\in R\\,|\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡0} \\\\  & ={x\\in R\\,|\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=1} \\\\  & ={…,-4\\pi ,-2\\pi ,0,2\\pi ,4\\pi ,…}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe see that $2\\pi $ is in $[0],$ while $3\\pi $ and $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ are not.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139534440 17592186045417 true] [17592186421723 1068 #edb/ref 17592186421706 13194139534440 1759218604541