421726 1071 "Consider the equivalence relation $∼$ on the set $A={0,1,2,…,9},$ defined by $a∼b$ if $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi a)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi b).$ How many distinct equivalence classes does $∼$ partition $A$ into?" 13194139534440 17592186045417 true] [17592186421726 1072 #uuid "d047cc89-db39-59a7-988f-ed7cc91aeaba" 13194139534440 17592186045417 true] [17592186421726 1074 "The equivalence classes of $∼$ partition $A,$ meaning that any element $a\\in A$ belongs to exactly one distinct equivalence class.\n\nSo, we compute equivalence classes until all the elements of $A$ have been placed into an equivalence class.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[0] & ={a\\in A∣a∼0} \\\\  & ={a\\in A∣\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi a)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi \\cdot 0)} \\\\  & ={a\\in A∣\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi a)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)} \\\\  & ={a\\in A∣\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi a)=1} \\\\  & ={0,2,4,6,8} \\\\  \\\\ [1] & ={a\\in A∣a∼1} \\\\  & ={a\\in A∣\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi a)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi \\cdot 1)} \\\\  & ={a\\in A∣\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi a)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi )} \\\\  & ={a\\in A∣\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi a)=-1} \\\\  & ={1,3,5,7,9} \\\\ \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAll of the elements of $A$ have been placed into an equivalence class, so we are done.\n\nWe have $2$ distinct equivalence classes:\n\n$$\n[0]={0,2,4,6,8},\\,[1]={1,3,5,7,9}.\n$$" 13194139534440 17592186045417 true] [17592186421727 1068 #edb/ref 17592186421713 13194139534440 17592186045417 true] [17592186421727 1069 "q-221743" 13194139534440 17592186045417 true] [17592186421727 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534440 17592186045417 true] [17592186421727 1071 "Consider the equivalence relation $∼$ on the set $A={0,\\,\\pm 1,\\,\\pm 2,\\,\\pm 3,\\,\\pm 4},$ defined by $x∼y$ if ${x}^{2}={y}^{2}.$ How many distinct equivalence classes does $∼$ partition $A$ into?" 13194139534440 17592186045417 true] [17592186421727 1072 #uuid "d77f9f0f-9817-528d-99c4-631fe49372a3" 13194139534440 17592186045417 true] [17592186421727 1074 "The equivalence classes of $∼$ partition $A,$ meaning that any element $a\\in A$ belongs to exactly one distinct equivalence class.\n\nSo, we compute equivalence classes until all the elements of $A$ have been placed into an equivalence class.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[0] & ={x\\in A∣x∼0} \\\\  & ={x\\in A∣{x}^{2}={0}^{2}} \\\\  & ={0} \\\\  \\\\ [1] & ={x\\in A∣x∼1} \\\\  & ={x\\in A∣{x}^{2}={1}^{2}} \\\\  & ={x\\in A∣{x}^{2}=1} \\\\  & ={-1,1} \\\\  \\\\ [2] & ={x\\in A∣x∼2} \\\\  & ={x\\in A∣{x}^{2}={2}^{2}} \\\\  & ={x\\in A∣{x}^{2}=4} \\\\  & ={-2,2} \\\\  \\\\ [3] & ={x\\in A∣x∼3} \\\\  & ={x\\in A∣{x}^{2}={3}^{2}} \\\\  & ={x\\in A∣{x}^{2}=9} \\\\  & ={-3,3} \\\\  \\\\ [4] & ={x\\in A∣x∼4} \\\\  & ={x\\in A∣{x}^{2}={4}^{2}} \\\\  & ={x\\in A∣{x}^{2}=16} \\\\  & ={-4,4} \\\\ \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAll of the elements of $A$ have been placed into an equivalence class, so we are done.\n\nWe have $5$ distinct equivalence classes:\n\n$$\n[0]={0},\\,[1]={-1,1},\\,[2]={-2,2},\\,[3]={-3,3},\\,[4]={-4,4}.\n$$\n\nNotice that the equivalence classes form a partition of $A,$ as expected." 13194139534440 17592186045417 true] [17592186421728 1068 #edb/ref 17592186421703 13194139534440 17592186045417 true] [17592186421728 1069 "q-221739" 13194139534440 17592186045417 true] [17592186421728 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534440 17592186045417 true] [17592186421728 1071 "Consider the equivalence relation $∼$ on the set $A={0,1,2,…,20},$ defined by $a∼b$ if $5∣(b-a).$   How many distinct equivalence classes does $∼$ partition $A$ into?" 13194139534440 17592186045417 true] [17592186421728 1072 #uuid "f558440e-fbbe-5f40-9ac9-a9ba45c6c38a" 13194139534440 17592186045417 true] [17592186421728 1074 "The equivalence classes of $∼$ partition $A,$ meaning that any element $a\\in A$ belongs to exactly one distinct equivalence class.\n\nSo, we compute equivalence classes until all the elements of $A$ have been placed into an equivalence class.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[0] & ={a\\in A\\,:\\,a∼0} \\\\  & ={a\\in A\\,:\\,5∣(a-0)} \\\\  & ={a\\in A\\,:\\,5∣a} \\\\  & ={0,5,10,15,20} \\\\  \\\\ [1] & ={a\\in A\\,:\\,a∼1} \\\\  & ={a\\in A\\,:\\,5∣(a-1)} \\\\  & ={1,6,11,16} \\\\  \\\\ [2] & ={a\\in A\\,:\\,a∼2} \\\\  & ={a\\in A\\,:\\,5∣(a-2)} \\\\  & ={2,7,12,17} \\\\  \\\\ [3] & ={a\\in A\\,:\\,a∼3} \\\\  & ={a\\in A\\,:\\,5∣(a-3)} \\\\  & ={3,8,13,18} \\\\  \\\\ [4] & ={a\\in A\\,:\\,a∼4} \\\\  & ={a\\in A\\,:\\,5∣(a-4)} \\\\  & ={4,9,14,19} \\\\ \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAll of the elements of $A$ have been placed into an equivalence class, so we are done.\n\nWe have $5$ distinct equivalence classes:\n\n$$\n[0]={0,5,10,15,20},\\,[1]={1,6,11,16},\\,[2]={2,7,12,17},\\,[3]={3,8,13,18},\\,[4]={4,9,14,19}.\n$$\n\nNotice that the equivalence classes form a partition of $A,$ as expected." 13194139534440 17592186045417 true] [17592186421729 1068 #edb/ref 17592186421707 13194139534440 17592186045417 true] [17592186421729 1069 "q-77214" 13194139534440 17592186045417 true] [17592186421729 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534440 17592186045417 true] [17592186421729 1071 "Consider the equivalence relation $∼$ on $Z,$ defined by $x∼y$ if $3\\,|\\,(x-y).$ Which of the following equivalence classes contain the element $5?$\n\nI. $[3]$\n\nII. $[-1]$\n\nIII. $[8]$" 13194139534440 17592186045417 true] [17592186421729 1072 #uuid "fd41a54f-8627-52be-9ff1-ad5548f86090" 13194139534440 17592186045417 true] [17592186421729 1074 "We have $5\\in [x]$ if $5∼x.$ Using the definition of the given equivalence relation, we require\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\,|\\,(5-x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now consider each of the given equivalence classes.\n\n- $5\\notin [3]$ because $3∤(5-3)\\,\\times $\n\n- $5\\in [-1]$ because $3\\,|\\,(5-(-1))\\,✓$\n\n- $5\\in [8]$ because $3\\,|\\,(5-8)\\,✓$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only\"." 13194139534440 17592186045417 true]] :edb/tempids {"answer-03e6d699-08b0-544b-9709-1cd54a9ffc8e" 17592186421632 "